# Frontend Type and Error Policy

When modifying frontend code, follow these typing and error-handling constraints to avoid sliding back into loose implementations.

## 1. DTOs and Mappers First
- When adding or changing backend response fields, update `web/src/types/api.ts` first.
- Put structural conversion or compatibility logic in `web/src/api/mappers/*`, not scattered across components or stores.
- When the backend adds optional enum/config-style metadata (e.g. race, alignment, realm) for frontend selectors, expose it via DTOs as `value + label` or structured entities; the frontend should not maintain its own hardcoded user-visible option lists.

## 2. Do Not Spread `any`
- New code must not introduce unnecessary `any`.
- If it must be used temporarily, state the transitional purpose and keep it to the smallest possible scope.

## 3. Single Exit for Error Handling
- Use `web/src/utils/appError.ts` (`logError` / `logWarn`) for logging.
- Show user-facing notices through the message layer (`message`) to avoid duplicate popups or inconsistent styles.

## 4. Layered Socket Message Handling
- `web/src/api/socket.ts` is responsible for transport only.
- Business dispatch of messages lives in `web/src/stores/socketMessageRouter.ts`; do not branch on message type inside components.

## 5. Frontend i18n Boundary
- New user-visible text in `.vue` files / composables must go through `vue-i18n` (strings under `web/src/locales/en-US/`) or backend-localized DTOs; do not hardcode fallback text.
- Component fallback text must also be localizable; e.g. when race is missing, do not fall back to a hardcoded `'Human'` — use `t(...)` or a default entity provided by the backend.
- When adding i18n keys for short slots such as titles, buttons, tabs, badges, status bar entries, and card titles, design them as short labels (provide `*_short` when needed) and put the full explanation in `*_desc`, tooltip, help, or body keys. Do not reuse one long `title` for the splash heading, status bar, narrow buttons, and dialog titles; the text must fit its real container while staying natural and accurate.
